--- body ---
## Context

Reported from an arena /molt sweep, 2026-11-08 (arena is a forge-less,
local-only repo; its forge adapter lists review records with string ids
such as `rv-20261101-t1211-retained-accounting-9284`).

`scripts/worktree-tmp-guard.py` `citation_sources()` raises
`Blocked("could not inspect citation sources (malformed PR record)")`
whenever a listed request's `number` is not an `int`. Every arena branch that
carries a local review record therefore fails closed in
`worktree-exit-preflight.sh --removal`, whatever its `.tmp/` holds.

Observed: 6 of 8 merged arena branches in clean worktrees (dead or no owner,
branch an ancestor of main) were blocked: t1192, t1198, t1211, t1215 and the
two docs/*-consent-20261031 branches, about 9.8 GB of `.tmp/` held. The two
branches with no review record passed and were removed.

Cause not established beyond the type check: the forge adapter (1319) may be
returning the documented shape for a local forge and the guard predates it,
or the adapter should normalise ids. Either side may be the fix.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] The guard accepts the request ids the forge adapter actually returns
  for a local forge (string ids) and still fails closed on a record lacking
  title/body, or the adapter returns a shape the guard accepts; one side
  owns the id type, documented.
- [ ] Test: a local-forge record with a string id that does not cite `.tmp/`
  lets removal proceed; one that cites a `.tmp/` path still blocks.

## Imagine (raid f1c4d5)

Premise holds on origin/main c65a9e32. The id type is already documented on
the adapter side: `scripts/forge.py` module doc says the none backend's
`number` "is an opaque review ID, not a numeric forge request" (review_notes
mints `rv-YYYYMMDD-<slug>-<hex>`). So the adapter owns the id type and the
guard is the stale side: fix the guard, not the adapter.

Simplest path: accept `number` as an `int` (gh) or as a `str` that is a single
safe path component (`^[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9._-]*$`), since
`registered_verdict_roots` joins it under `.panel/` and `.review/`. Anything
else, including a missing or non-string title or body, an empty id, or an id
holding `/` or `..`, stays fail-closed as a malformed record. No new
abstraction.

## Plan

Actions:
1. `tests/test_worktree_tools.py`: next to the existing int-number fake listing
   (around line 267), add two removal-mode tests with a local-forge style
   record `{"number": "rv-20261101-t1211-x-9284", ...}`: (a) the body does not
   cite `.tmp/`, so removal proceeds; (b) the body cites the worktree's
   `.tmp/` path, so removal blocks with a citation. Add a third check: a record
   with a string id but no `body` still blocks as malformed, and so does an id
   containing `/`.
2. `scripts/worktree-tmp-guard.py` `citation_sources()`: widen the id check as
   above, and write a one-line comment citing forge.py as the owner of the id
   type. Label the source `request <id> body` (or keep `PR #`) for both shapes.
3. Run the new tests by name and see them fail first, then pass; then run the
   whole `tests/test_worktree_tools.py`.

First test: (a), a string id without a citation must let removal proceed. Today
it raises "malformed PR record", which is the targeted failure.

Feasibility: PASS. Paths and lines checked: guard lines 212-219, forge.py
doc line 24, test fixture line 267. No cross-ticket conflict (single ticket).
